  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday) \nIssued at 135 AM MDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nThe mid and upper level dry air will continue to push into NM on\nWednesday, disrupting the monsoon plume further, though PWATs do\nnot decrease significantly. NBM PoPs appear to be much too low and \nisolated, if not scattered, storms should still develop over the \nhigh terrain. The RRFS is the most aggressive model, showing \nstorm coverage very similar to Tuesday. Though PoPs were increased\nfrom the NBM, PoPs may increase further as we get closer to \nWednesday.\n\nAdditionally, by Wednesday we should see a tropical system just\nsouth of the Baja. Though the ridge axis will still extend \nwestward into NM, moisture associated with the tropical system \nshould begin to shift northward on Thursday toward AZ and NM and \nspread across at least western NM by Friday. This should increase \nprecipitation chances once again, especially by Friday. Of \ncourse, as is usually the case with moisture from tropical \norigins, it tends to bring more cloud cover, more stability, and \nhence more stratiform rain rather than heavy thunderstorm rains. \nTime will tell, but models are already hinting at this. \nUnfortunately, the moisture will have a difficult time making much\neastward progress in NM as the pesky upper high will have begun \nto move back toward NM. By Friday, the high center should be \naround eastern KS or MO, and by Saturday over central or western \nKS. Unfortunately, both the EC and GFS show the high strengthening\nas it continues to inch westward on Sunday. Therefore, \nprecipitation chances Friday through the weekend will favor areas \nalong and west of the Central Mtn Chain, while eastern NM will be\nhot and mostly dry.",
